WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.530
NARRATOR: Congratulations.

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:04.050
Your Kubernetes application
is in production,

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:07.150
and the number of services
you own are growing.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:09.310
But as you scale your
services in production,

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:11.820
you also need to
scale your monitoring.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.550
After all, you can't fix
what you don't monitor,

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.020
and your monitoring
needs to be as

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.090
reliable as your application.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:21.060
Prometheus is the standard
Kubernetes monitoring system,

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.660
which helps you collect,
store, query, and alert

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.150
on metrics important
to your applications

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:28.740
and infrastructure.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.770
As you scale up, it can become
challenging to configure

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:35.100
and manage Prometheus to keep
up with the volume of metrics

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.360
from all of your
successful services.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.310
This can be a thankless task.

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:41.610
After all, your users don't
care about how you manage

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.230
your monitoring infrastructure.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.780
They care about making
sure they can use your app.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.150
Google Cloud Managed
Service for Prometheus

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.760
is designed to help you
scale your Prometheus-based

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:51.930
monitoring.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:55.110
It's fully managed by Google,
removing operational toil

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:58.760
from tasks like charting
Prometheus servers,

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:01.910
managing complex
scrape configurations,

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:04.730
and federating Prometheus
servers to make a global data

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:06.080
source.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.140
By using the same planet-scale
time series database

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:11.570
that Google uses for
its own monitoring,

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:13.610
Managed Service for
Prometheus enables

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:17.060
users to collect metrics at
incredible scale with zero

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.100
operational overhead.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.890
This puts global monitoring
and alerting within reach,

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:25.220
enabling you to gather metrics
from all of your services

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:26.630
across the planet.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:29.210
Metrics collection
within your GKE clusters

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:32.630
also becomes easier as
Managed Service for Prometheus

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:34.730
provides managed
collectors that you can

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:36.500
run in your various clusters.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.920
These no longer need to maintain
state in your own environment,

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.280
as they are built to write to
and store metrics in Managed

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.230
Service for Prometheus.

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:47.870
All this said, Managed
Service for Prometheus

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:51.380
is an in-place replacement for
running your own Prometheus

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:54.680
servers, which means you
can keep using your existing

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:58.190
Prometheus alerts and your
existing Grafana dashboards.

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:01.700
Managed Service for Prometheus
supports any application that

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:04.320
uses PromQL to query metrics.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.260
You can also utilize
its integrations

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.150
with Google Cloud Monitoring.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:11.130
To get started, you'll
need to take two steps.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.850
First, enable Managed
Service for Prometheus

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:17.870
in your GKE cluster and
deploy Prometheus collectors.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:20.990
These can be deployed alongside
your existing Prometheus

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:22.630
servers.

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:24.250
These collectors
will be configured

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.790
to write metrics directly to
Managed Service for Prometheus.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:31.630
Second, connect clients, such
as Grafana or other tools you

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:34.930
may use, to read Prometheus
metrics from Managed Service

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:36.970
for Prometheus.

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:38.020
And that's it.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:39.940
With Managed Service
for Prometheus,

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.850
now you're ready to spend less
time configuring monitoring

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:46.300
infrastructure and more time
adding the next big feature

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:47.290
to your app.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:48.890
Want to learn more?

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:50.860
Check out more details
in the links below.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.210
[MUSIC PLAYING]

